Chapter: VirtioRng
  cites Foreword chapter VirtioBlk

 The transport is the one VirtioBlk drives and its constants are reused
 by name; what differs is the device's, fixed by the VIRTIO specification
 and not derivable from this code. Device id 4 is the entropy device. It
 has one virtqueue, requestq, and no feature bits this driver needs. A
 request is a single device-writable buffer with no header and no status
 byte: the device fills it with entropy and reports through the used ring
 how many bytes it wrote, which may be fewer than asked.

  vrng-device-id : Integer = 4

Section: Probing

  vrng-slot-is-rng : Integer -> Boolean
  vrng-slot-is-rng (base) =
   if vb-read base vb-off-magic /= vb-magic then False
   else if vb-read base vb-off-version /= vb-version-modern then False
   else vb-read base vb-off-device-id == vrng-device-id

  vrng-find-loop : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  vrng-find-loop (base) (stride) (count) (i) =
   if i >= count then 0
   else if vrng-slot-is-rng (base + i * stride) then base + i * stride
   else vrng-find-loop base stride count (i + 1)

  vrng-find : Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  vrng-find (base) (stride) (count) = vrng-find-loop base stride count 0

Section: Initialisation and the ready cache

  vrng-init : Integer, Integer -> Integer
  vrng-init (base) (ring) = vb-init base ring

  vrng-ready : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  vrng-ready (mmio) (stride) (count) (ring) =
   let cache = ring + vb-cache-offset
   in let cached = bit-and (peek-32 cache 0) #FFFFFFFF
   in if cached /= 0 then cached
   else let dev = vrng-find mmio stride count
   in if dev == 0 then 0
   else if vrng-init dev ring == 0 then 0
   else let w = poke-32 cache 0 dev
   in dev

Section: One request

 The used ring holds, after its 2-byte flags and 2-byte index, one
 8-byte element per completed request: a 32-bit descriptor id and the
 32-bit count of bytes the device wrote. The element for the request the
 driver just made sits at the index the used counter reached, masked to
 the queue.

  vrng-used-len : Integer, Integer -> Integer
  vrng-used-len (ring) (used-idx) =
   let slot = bit-and (used-idx - 1) (vb-queue-size - 1)
   in bit-and (peek-32 (ring + vb-ring-used + 4 + slot * 8 + 4) 0) #FFFFFFFF

  vrng-request : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  vrng-request (base) (ring) (buf) (n) =
   let d0 = ring + vb-ring-desc
   in let e0 = vb-put-desc d0 buf n vb-desc-write 0
   in let a = ring + vb-ring-avail
   in let cur = vb-ring-idx a
   in let want = bit-and (vb-ring-idx (ring + vb-ring-used) + 1) #FFFF
   in let slot = vb-put-avail-entry (a + 4 + (bit-and cur (vb-queue-size - 1)) * 2) 0
   in let bump = vb-set-avail-idx a (cur + 1)
   in let notify = vb-write base vb-off-queue-notify 0
   in if vb-await-used ring want 0 == 0 then 0
   else vrng-used-len ring want

Section: The door

 The only function with a row, for the reason VirtioBlk gives at its own
 door: a citing program declares Device, the ARM64 runtime enforces it at
 the address, and a row on the inner functions would be an instrument over
 a hole. Answers the bytes written into buf, or 0 when no entropy device is
 on the bus, the handshake was refused, or the request never completed.

  vrng-read-auto : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> [Device.Mmio] Integer
  vrng-read-auto (mmio) (stride) (count) (ring) (buf) (n) =
   let dev = vrng-ready mmio stride count ring
   in if dev == 0 then 0
   else vrng-request dev ring buf n
